From 9ee218fdb8b5fb9f2c56e066be5eeda0efeb953f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:34:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][DVBridge] The GPU time of each pass, one frame in
 eight, without a setting

What each part of a frame costs on a GPU (the passes of the bridge,
the composition of Dolby Vision and its enhancement layer, the
upscaler, the mapping to the display) was measured only with
<video><dvpasstimes>true</dvpasstimes></video> in advancedsettings.xml,
which needs SSH or Samba: a Ryzen 5 3400GE at 100 % could not be read
from its report (LibreELEC forum, thread 30682, post 195).

The bridge now times its passes on any GPU that has
GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query, in one frame of eight
(CDVBridgeGLES::PASS_SAMPLE_EVERY): SamplePasses(), as each frame
begins to be drawn, tells the renderer whether this frame is timed
(dvbridge_render_sample_passes()), and a frame that is not begins no
query, neither for libplacebo's passes (pl_opengl_pause_timers(),
libplacebo-9947) nor for the bridge's own. The times already taken
are read back as they come, as before. dvpasstimes now times every
frame.

- Native Dolby Vision: the 10-s line of kodi.log and the row "GPU by
  pass" of the System page of the player information have the passes,
  and the line says one frame in how many was timed.
- The conversion bridge (Dolby Vision converted to HDR10 or SDR, HDR10
  adapted to the display, the upscaler): a 10-s line of its own in
  kodi.log ("pictures of the conversion bridge ... GPU time by pass"),
  and the same row of the System page (hades.dv.passes, from its last
  window). The upscaler's line no longer takes them.
- libplacebo leaves GL_GPU_DISJOINT_EXT to Kodi with the timers on:
  a disjoint state now also drops the conversion bridge's times, read
  where no timer of Kodi reads it (Dolby Vision converted, and HDR
  mapped to SDR without the upscaler).

A diagnostic report has kodi.log, and so the passes.
